WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.013
So let's start off with

00:00:01.013 --> 00:00:05.370
that first seminal Steven
Blair paper from 1989 in JAMA,

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.980
in that they had about 10,000 men

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.200
and about 3,000 women or so.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.390
And what's actually
interesting about this study,

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:13.920
and many others like it,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.220
they typically follow the
individuals for years.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.270
I believe in this actual study,

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.920
it was something like nine years,

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.370
and within that, several
hundred people actually died.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.290
And so it's a bit morbid, I understand,

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.050
but it makes the science
incredibly compelling

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.080
because we can look at a number of people,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.670
wait for several of them to die,

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:33.503
and then come back and say,

00:00:33.503 --> 00:00:35.910
"What actually was different
between those people who died

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.790
at baseline versus those who didn't die?

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.380
You know, again, at
baseline and after that."

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.900
And so we can get really
strong insights about

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.730
what predicted death.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.230
Now what they found in this initial study,

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.850
and this is directly
from the paper itself,

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.100
was after age adjustment,

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.920
so again, they would kind
of factor in their age,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.540
and say, "Let's take that
out of the equation."

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.810
So after age-adjusted,
all-cause mortality,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.580
meaning died for any reason,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.430
declined directly across fitness levels.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.740
So as you reduced your fitness,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.590
you increased your
all-cause mortality risk.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.680
And it went from a number of
what is referred to as 64,

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.173
so 64 deaths per 10,000 people,

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.540
that was the highest rate there.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.710
It reduced from that to about 18.6.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.440
And so again, if you're looking
at that saying, "All right,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.571
if I go from the least-fit
category to the most-fit,

00:01:24.571 --> 00:01:29.100
my risk goes from 64
deaths per 10,000 people,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.400
down to 18 deaths per 10,000 people."

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.420
If that part has confused you,

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.630
just run the 18 versus the 64.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:37.950
So another way to think about that is

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.470
if my risk of dying is 18,

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.080
and now it all of a
sudden, it goes up to 64,

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.090
it's a huge increase in your risk of dying

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.230
and nobody wants that.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.600
Similar story for the women,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:49.680
the numbers there actually went

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.040
from the risk per 10,000 was 39.5,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.920
and reduced all the way to 8.5.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.020
And so again, clear evidence

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.100
that this thing was happening.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.420
And what's also interesting here,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.760
just because someone will ask, I'm sure,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.770
this was true once they factored out

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.900
things like, again, as I mentioned age,

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.200
but also smoking habits,
cholesterol levels,

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.380
systolic blood pressure,
fasting blood glucose levels,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.590
parental history of
coronary artery disease,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.807
and then follow up, and other metrics.

00:02:18.807 --> 00:02:20.130
And so what they're basically saying is,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.440
even if you take those
things into account,

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.520
you still see this massive
reduction in health

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.770
when we have a reduction
in cardiovascular fitness.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.420
Now I realize following numbers like that

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.830
is sometimes difficult

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.600
if you're only listening to
this in the audio version,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.180
so we'll have this
paper in the show notes.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.393
The actual title of the paper is

00:02:37.393 --> 00:02:40.050
" Physical Fitness and
All-Cause Mortality:

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.840
A Prospective Study of
Healthy Men and Women."

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.690
And again, first author
Steven Blair from 1989.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.050
So if you cruise onto
Table 2 of that paper,

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:52.320
you're going to see that they
actually ran the analysis

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.560
and split up the men and
women into Quintiles.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.040
So this would mean the
lowest 20% of fitness,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.350
the next 20, next 20,
next 20, the next 20.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.740
So take everyone across the
spectrum, lowest to highest,

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.870
and split 'em up, top 20%,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.640
et cetera, et cetera, all the way down.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.160
And what I'll read off to
you is the relative risk.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.500
And again, this is risk of dying,

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.470
as we go from the most-fit 20 percentile,

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.300
to the next most-fit,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.580
to the middle kind of 20%,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.170
to the second to last 20%,

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.760
all the way to the bottom 20%,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.890
that's the way to view this.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.020
So if you start at the
highest level of fitness

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.200
and we put that as just
a number of 1.0, right?

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.570
So this is a saying,
"Okay, you're at a 1.0."

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.270
if I go from the top 20 percentile

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.830
to the next 20 percentile,

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.640
so think of this as like 60
to 80th percent, if you will.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.820
My risk goes from 1 to 1.7.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.150
So this is a 17% increase in risk.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:49.980
If I go to the next one down,

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:54.980
it's gone from 1 to 1.7 to 1.46.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.750
The next after that, 1.36,

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.880
and then here's where it explodes.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.110
So again, think of this as

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.740
if you are somewhere between
the 20th to 40th percentile,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.470
100 being the best, 0
being the absolute worst,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.980
so just being the second to last category,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.500
your risk is 1.37.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.220
You go from that category to
the bottom 20th percentile,

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.050
so just one category below,

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:23.640
your risk goes from 1.37 to 3.44.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.080
And this is why people will highlight,

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.560
you don't have to necessarily
be the fittest on the planet

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.290
from a health and
cardiovascular risk perspective,

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.273
but you cannot be the lowest.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.820
The magnitude of improvement you see

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.820
from going from the
least-fit people around,

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.103
to just a second least-fit,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:46.380
is almost half to three
times the risk reduction.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:47.760
So massive improvements,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.000
you'll see the exact same
thing in women in this study.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.130
So to not run you through everything,

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.450
but we really talking about

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.110
improvements as you go from
the healthiest or most-fit,

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:59.100
kind of going all the way down

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.930
to the bottom 20th percentile,

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.823
that risk factor is 2.42,

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.480
and then the lowest goes from 2.42,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.150
all the way up to 4.65.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.310
So a similar message between
the men and the women,

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.470
just being the bottom of that category

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.680
is incredibly dangerous and
problematic for your health,

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.560
so if you can just do a little
bit to bump up one level,

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.480
that's going to do a lot for you.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.950
Now again, there's a ton of studies

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:23.783
you could pull from here,

00:05:23.783 --> 00:05:24.960
the numbers, again,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.520
I don't want you to be
super specific on that

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.700
'cause they will differ
depending upon the population.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.353
A little bit of context on that,

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:35.370
I grabbed another study
for you also in JAMA

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:36.330
far more recently

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.390
called "The Association of
Cardio Respiratory Fitness

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:42.720
with Long-Term Mortality Among Adults

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.600
Undergoing Exercise Treadmill Testing."

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.020
And this is actually going
to tell you a similar story,

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:49.950
but I wanted to show you

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.170
how even the studies that
are a little bit different

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.030
are going to have the
same take-home message.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.590
In this particular analysis,

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.800
now they've got over 122,000 patients.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.430
So okay, great, maybe there was
something unique about Blair

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.950
and his little population
of 15,000 people, or so,

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.980
what about if we 10 x that number roughly,

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.480
do we see the same basic results?

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.190
And the answer is effectively yes.

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.970
So in this, almost 14,000 people died

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:16.650
throughout the course of this study.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:18.600
So we're getting same kind of idea,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:19.590
pretty healthy people,

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:20.850
some are going to die,

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.110
but what does it really look like

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.310
in terms of the folks that stayed alive

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.260
and those that did not?

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:26.850
I'll zoom you all the way down to the end

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.030
to not make it so painstaking
as the previous one,

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:31.320
but similar stuff here,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.750
in fact it's even more jarring

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.330
because they're able to do
more in-depth analysis here

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:37.830
of some of those other co-factors,

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.630
which is what I want to highlight, so.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.157
You know, directly from
the paper again here,

00:06:42.157 --> 00:06:44.280
"The increase in all-cause mortality

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.870
is associated with reduced
cardiorespiratory fitness,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.000
which was comparable or to or greater

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.700
than traditional clinical risk factors,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:58.410
such as coronary artery
disease, smoking, and diabetes."

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.510
Now I am certainly not trying to tell you

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:01.770
that as long as you're in shape

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.600
that it's okay if you
smoke or do anything else,

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.570
I have nothing of expertise whatsoever

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.450
in your actual clinical health,

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.300
work through that with your
actual doctor or physician.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.850
Again, just from this
one particular study,

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.980
really profound there, right?

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:20.130
The cardiovascular
fitness, again, VO₂ Max,

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:21.670
was more predictive

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.260
than traditional risk factors
like coronary artery disease,

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.150
smoking, and diabetes, right?

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:28.800
So I'll put numbers behind this

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.700
because it gets even more interesting.

00:07:31.537 --> 00:07:34.050
"The cardiorespiratory fitness
is inversely associated

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.270
with long-term mortality

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:38.910
and not observed to be an upper limit."

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.293
What that also means is,

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:45.670
there doesn't seem to be
any reduction in the benefit

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.020
by continuing to increase your VO₂ Max.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:49.853
So in other words,

00:07:49.853 --> 00:07:51.750
the higher your VO₂ Max goes,

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.900
the more it seems to preserve
all-cause mortality risk.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.610
So there doesn't appear
to be in this study,

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:58.260
in fact, you'd see the same thing

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.050
if you looked at almost any
other study in this area,

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.060
there seems to be no upper limit.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:04.837
And so there's just really
not a rationale of saying,

00:08:04.837 --> 00:08:07.050
"Well, I'm good enough here,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.460
I'm okay, this is enough,

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.500
and if I get any better it
won't really help that much."

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.750
You actually do see that
in sport performance.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.700
So a classic example here is

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:16.590
in the sport of mixed martial arts,

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.300
if you were to examine the VO₂ Max's

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.260
of the athletes in that,

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.730
you would see that it's
kind of on an average

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:23.340
of about 55 milliliters
per kilogram per minute,

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.260
and if you get past that,

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:29.940
the benefits of performance
continue to go up, but slightly,

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.820
and once you really start
getting past north of 65,

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:35.670
it seems to be really no more association

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:37.170
between improved performance,

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:38.820
by that I mean winning fights,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.460
it doesn't mean it is
detrimental, of course,

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.490
or not advantageous to
be in better fitness

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.320
prior to a mixed martial art fight,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.290
but we're just saying the rate
of increase in performance

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.180
against the rate of increase in VO₂ Max

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.680
starts to taper off.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.470
We don't see a similar thing
with cardiovascular health.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.030
So hopefully, I've made my point here,

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.400
about the importance of VO₂ Max,

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.420
but in case I haven't,

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.160
just one more final study

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.300
that I thought was of interest here,

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.880
to bring this point home even more.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.730
Actually, this is one of Jonathan
Myers's more recent papers

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:11.250
called "Cardiorespiratory
Fitness and Mortality Risk

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:14.250
Across the Spectra of Age, Race, and Sex,"

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.860
published in the last
couple of years here,

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.510
and this is actually
in 750,000 US veterans

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:23.250
between the ages of 30 and 95,

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:26.580
and I like this paper because
the sample size is enormous.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.860
Again, it takes into
account things like race,

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:30.930
as well as age at that spectrum, right?

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:32.910
Almost a 65-year spectrum.

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:37.910
And within that, 175,000
people or so died.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.560
So if it held up against 15,000,

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.780
then it held up against 150,000

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.510
and now it's holding up against 750,000,

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:46.343
I just don't know

00:09:46.343 --> 00:09:48.390
how much more evidence
one would need to see

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:51.780
to believe not only in
this as an actual finding,

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:53.250
but the relative risk ratio

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:56.100
seems to be lining up
across all these studies

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.110
as pretty similar.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:00.000
And so what they found in
this, again, same idea,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.630
they found no reduced
benefit of extreme fitness.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.250
In other words, the higher the VO₂ Max,

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:06.960
just the higher the risk reduction,

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:08.960
there seemed to be no upper limit there.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.510
And in addition, what they
found was a couple of metrics.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:13.890
So if you take into account

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:15.900
what are called the comorbidities,

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:18.690
and so you look at things like diabetes.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:20.340
Diabetes in this particular study,

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:23.970
took their risk factor from 1 to 1.34,

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.650
so that's a big deal.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:30.300
However, going from the
highest fitness level

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:33.150
to the next highest fitness level,

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.393
represented an increase of risk of 1.66.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:39.690
So again, I'm not saying
that diabetes is okay

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:40.680
or anything like that,

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.500
again, I'm not a medical doctor,

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.110
and I don't do really
anything with disease,

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:47.790
but just look how staggering this is.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:49.380
And in fact, if you run
this all the way out,

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:50.610
examples in this paper,

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.070
and here I'm looking at
Figure 2, by the way,

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:54.870
in case you want to go look yourself,

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:58.260
you're talking about the
addition of age represented

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:02.040
a 1.06 increase in risk factor,

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:05.220
hypertension, smoking,
arterial fibrillation, cancer,

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:06.270
all these things are plotted,

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.910
and you can see how much
they increased risk,

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.820
and all of those, the highest one

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:15.183
was chronic kidney disease,
which represented 1.49.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:18.813
When you look into the VO₂ Max numbers,

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:24.450
the lowest risk factor was 1.39.

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.070
And then it just escalates from there,

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:30.160
to 1.66, to 2.1, to 2.9, again
with the least-fit people

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:33.723
having a 4X higher risk of mortality,

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:36.720
that's how important your heart is.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.660
And so it's hard for me
to make a cogent argument,

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.750
that even as athletes
who are interested in,

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.040
say again, dunking a basketball

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:48.180
or these anaerobic high
power, low fatigue sports,

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.020
very difficult to say, your
heart is not playing a big role

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:53.250
in your global health,

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:54.420
and that that isn't going to limit

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.963
your performances somehow.
[music]

